L000017	\begin{document}
L000018	
L000019	\title{Landau-Zener tunneling and quantum interference of Andreev states}
L000020	\author{Mikhail S. Kalenkov}
L000021	\affiliation{I.E.Tamm Department of Theoretical Physics, P.N.Lebedev Physical Institute, 119991 Moscow, Russia}
L000022	\author{Andrei D. Zaikin}
L000023	\affiliation{I.E.Tamm Department of Theoretical Physics, P.N.Lebedev Physical Institute, 119991 Moscow, Russia}
L000024	\affiliation{National Research University Higher School of Economics, 101000 Moscow, Russia}
L000025	
L000026	\date{\today}
L000027	\begin{abstract}
L000028	With the aid of a microscopic theory we derive an effective Hamiltonian that controls quantum dynamics of Andreev states in superconducting nanojunctions out of equilibrium. Resolving the corresponding Schr\"odinger-like equation we obtain the "wave functions" for Andreev levels and evaluate electric current across the junction in the presence of an external voltage bias. We demonstrate that quantum interference of Andreev states may yield pronounced coherent oscillations of the supercurrent as a function of the Josephson phase in junctions with barrier transmissions slightly below unity. Further implications of this novel effect are expected for junctions with diffusive barriers.
L000029	\end{abstract}
L000030	
L000031	\maketitle
L000032	
L000033	{\it Introduction.} Josephson effects \cite{Jos} in superconducting junctions with low transmission tunnel barriers are well understood \cite{BP}.
L000034	The same applies to equilibrium properties of superconducting weak links at arbitrary transmissions \cite{KO,KGI}. The situation becomes more complicated if the system is driven out of equilibrium, e.g., by an externally applied bias voltage $V$. Theoretical analysis of ac Josephson effect is successful in junctions at full transmissions \cite{Uwe,AB1,AB2} demonstrating qualitatively new features as compared to the standard tunneling limit \cite{BP}.
L000035	
L000036	What if the barrier transmission is only slightly below unity? An attempt to answer this question was made by Averin and Bardas \cite{AB1} employing a physical picture of Landau-Zener tunneling between two subgap Andreev levels schematically illustrated in Fig. 1. In this way they evaluated the time-dependent supercurrent $I$ across the junction in the form \cite{AB1}
L000037	
L000038	\begin{figure}
L000039	\begin{center}
L000040	\includegraphics[width=8cm]{ABS-R1-003-R2-003-theta-0-fig}
L000041	\end{center}
L000042	\caption{Phase dependent energy of subgap Andreev levels in superconducting junctions at high transmissions.}
L000043	\label{ABS-fig}
L000044	\end{figure}
L000045	
L000046	\begin{equation}
L000047	I
L000048	= I_c\sin \Bigl[\frac{\chi }{2}\Bigr] \sgn V
L000049	\begin{cases}
L000050	1, & 0<\chi <\pi,
L000051	\\
L000052	[2e^{-\frac{\pi R|\Delta |}{eV}}-1], & \pi<\chi <2\pi,
L000053	\end{cases}
L000054	\label{Av}
L000055	\end{equation}
L000056	where $I_c$ is the junction critical current, $R \ll 1$ is its barrier reflection coefficient, $\Delta$ denotes the superconducting order parameter and $\chi (t)=2eVt$ defines the time dependent Josephson phase difference in the presence of a constant voltage $V$ applied to the junction. The current-phase relation (CPR) \eqref{Av} naturally reduces to that at full transmissions \cite{AB1,AB2} in the limit $R \to 0$ and to the low temperature equilibrium result \cite{KO} in the limit $V \to 0$. Here and below we set Planck constant $\hbar$ equal to unity.
L000057	
L000058	More recently, Galaktionov and one of the authors \cite{GZ1} reconsidered the problem by performing a regular perturbative expansion in $R \ll 1$ within the standard physical picture involving the concept of multiple Andreev reflections (MAR) \cite{MAR}. While the time average current value $\overline{I}$ following from the formula \eqref{Av} was reproduced, CPR obtained within the applicability range of the perturbation theory  \cite{GZ1} turned out different from that in Eq. \eqref{Av}.
L000059	
L000060	Turning back to the issue of Landau-Zener tunneling, we point out that its standard quantum mechanical treatment is not sufficient here at least for two reasons. Firstly, Andreev states correspond to a non-trivial superposition of quasiparticles and holes and, hence, cannot in general be treated as ordinary single particle levels. Secondly, in the presence of any non-zero voltage bias Andreev states demonstrate {\it non-unitary} time evolution \cite{KZ} generally implying both dissipation and decoherence.
L000061	
L000062	In this Letter we are going to microscopically derive an effective Hamiltonian and formulate a Schr\"odinger-like equation that allows to successfully overcome the abovementioned problems. Within the framework of this physical picture -- which is complementary to (and fully consistent with) that of MAR -- we will evaluate electric current across the system and demonstrate that at low but non-zero values of $R$ CPR of a voltage-biased superconducting junction may exhibit pronounced coherent oscillations caused by quantum interference between Andreev states.
L000063	
L000064	
L000065	{\it The model and effective Hamiltonian.}
L000066	We will consider the standard model for a single channel superconducting junction with transmission $D=1-R$ biased by an external voltage $V(t)$. This problem can be treated microscopically within the framework of the quasiclassical Eilenberger-Keldysh equations \cite{BWBSZ} supplemented by Zaitsev boundary conditions \cite{Zaitsev}. As a result, one arrives at a formally exact expression for electric current, which can be written in a compact form \cite{KZ}
L000067	\begin{multline}
L000068	I(t)=
L000069	ie
L000070	\Sp\Bigl\{
L000071	[\tilde W +  a ]^{-1}
L000072	[h - a h a^+]
L000073	\left([\tilde W +  a ]^{-1}\right)^+
L000074	\\
L000075	\times\tilde W^+[\chi(t)] \partial_{\chi} \tilde W[\chi(t)]
L000076	\Bigr\},
L000077	\label{T3}
L000078	\end{multline}
L000079	where all products are understood as time convolutions,
L000080	$a(\varepsilon) = -\varepsilon/|\Delta| + \sqrt{(\varepsilon/|\Delta|)^2 - 1}$ is the energy dependent Andreev amplitude,
L000081	$h(\varepsilon) = \tanh(\varepsilon/2T)$, and we defined
L000082	\begin{equation}
L000083	\tilde W(\chi)  =
L000084	\begin{pmatrix}
L000085	W_d(\chi) & W_o(\chi) \\
L000086	-W_o(\chi) & W_d(\chi)
L000087	\end{pmatrix}
L000088	\label{tW}
L000089	\end{equation}
L000090	with $W_d =\sqrt{D} \cos (\chi/2) \hat p_3+ \sqrt{R}\hat p_1$, $W_o = \sqrt{D} \sin (\chi/2) \hat 1$. Here and below $\hat p_{1,2,3}$ are Pauli matrices and $\hat1$ is $2\times2$ unity matrix.
L000091	
L000092	
L000093	 The task at hand is to evaluate the inverse operator $(\tilde W +  a)^{-1}$ expressed in the following exact form
L000094	\begin{multline}
L000095	(\tilde W +  a)^{-1}
L000096	=
L000097	-\dfrac{|\Delta|}{2}
L000098	\begin{pmatrix}
L000099	(\varepsilon - \mathcal{H})^{-1} & 0 \\
L000100	0 & (\varepsilon - \mathcal{H})^{-1}
L000101	\end{pmatrix}
L000102	\\
L000103	\times\Biggl[
L000104	\begin{pmatrix}
L000105	W_o a^{-1} W_o^{-1} & 0
L000106	\\
L000107	0 & W_o a^{-1} W_o^{-1}
L000108	\end{pmatrix} \tilde W^+ + 1
L000109	\Biggr]
L000110	\label{inv2}
L000111	\end{multline}
L000112	with an effective Hamiltonian
L000113	\begin{gather}
L000114	\mathcal{H} =
L000115	\dfrac{|\Delta|}{2}
L000116	\Bigl[
L000117	W_d
L000118	+
L000119	W_o a^{-1}  W_o^{-1} W_d a
L000120	+
L000121	W_o a^{-1}  W_o^{-1}
L000122	-
L000123	a^{-1}
L000124	\Bigr].
L000125	\label{Heff}
L000126	\end{gather}
L000127	It will be convenient for us to restrict our analysis to the Josephson phase values within the interval $0 <\chi (t) < 2\pi$. With sufficient accuracy (see End Matter) the expression for the Hamiltonian \eqref{Heff} can be reduced to
L000128	\begin{gather}
L000129	\mathcal{H} =
L000130	\mathcal{H}_0
L000131	+
L000132	\dfrac{i\dot \chi(t)}{4}\hat 1
L000133	\cot \Bigl[\frac{\chi(t)}{2}\Bigr]
L000134	-
L000135	\dfrac{\dot \chi(t)}{4}
L000136	\hat p_3
L000137	\label{Heff3}
L000138	\end{gather}
L000139	with $\mathcal{H}_0 =|\Delta|W_d$ and $\dot \chi(t)=2eV(t) \ll |\Delta |$.
L000140	
L000141	In order to evaluate the operator $(\varepsilon - \mathcal{H})^{-1}$ let us obtain a general solution of the equation $i \partial \Psi/\partial t = \mathcal{H} \Psi$. Making use of a substitution
L000142	\begin{equation}
L000143	\Psi =\sqrt{\sin [\chi(t)/2]}\tilde \Psi,
L000144	\label{subst}
L000145	\end{equation}
L000146	we arrive at a Schr\"odinger-like equation
L000147	\begin{equation}
L000148	i \dfrac{\partial \tilde \Psi}{\partial t}
L000149	=\tilde{\mathcal{H}}
L000150	\tilde \Psi, \quad
L000151	\tilde{\mathcal{H}}
L000152	=
L000153	\mathcal{H}_0
L000154	-
L000155	\dfrac{\dot \chi(t)}{4}\hat p_3
L000156	\label{shrod}
L000157	\end{equation}
L000158	with the Hermitian effective Hamiltonian $\tilde{\mathcal{H}}$. Note that linear in $\dot \chi(t)$ terms must be retained here: Although small, they accumulate over long times leading to non-negligible changes of the "wave function". We also point out that -- although technically our formalism does not require this limitation -- for the sake of simplicity below we restrict our analysis to the limit of sufficiently slowly varying in time bias voltage which -- if one so wishes -- can then be replaced by a constant $V(t)=V$.
L000159	
L000160	It is convenient to introduce two eigenvectors of the matrix $\mathcal{H}_0$ corresponding to two subgap Andreev bound
L000161	states with energies
L000162	$\pm \varepsilon_A(\chi)$
L000163	\begin{equation}
L000164	\mathcal{H}_0(\chi) \varphi_{\pm}(\chi) = \pm \varepsilon_A(\chi)\varphi_{\pm}(\chi),
L000165	\label{H0}
L000166	\end{equation}
L000167	where $\varepsilon_A(\chi)=|\Delta|\sqrt{1 - D \sin^2(\chi/2)}$.
L000168	The eigenvectors $\varphi_{\pm}(\chi)$ can be identified in terms of a single real vector
L000169	\begin{equation}
L000170	\varphi(\chi)
L000171	=
L000172	\dfrac{
L000173	\begin{pmatrix}
L000174	\sqrt{R} |\Delta | \\
L000175	\varepsilon_A(\chi) - \sqrt{D} |\Delta | \cos (\chi/2)
L000176	\end{pmatrix}}{
L000177	\sqrt{2 \varepsilon_A(\chi) \left[ \varepsilon_A(\chi) - \sqrt{D} |\Delta | \cos (\chi/2)\right]}
L000178	}
L000179	\label{vector}
L000180	\end{equation}
L000181	by means of the equations
L000182	$\varphi_{+} = \varphi(\chi)$ and $\varphi_{-} = i \hat  p_2 \varphi(\chi)$. Far from the anticrossing point  $\chi(t_{\pi}) = \pi$ Eq. \eqref{shrod} can  be solved within an adiabatic approximation with the result
L000183	\begin{gather}
L000184	\psi_{\pm}(t) =
L000185	\dfrac{\varphi_{\pm}[\chi(t)]}{\sqrt{a[\pm\varepsilon_A[\chi(t)]]}} \exp\left(
L000186	\mp i \int_{t_{\pi}}^t\varepsilon_A[\chi(t')] d t'
L000187	\right)
L000188	\label{psit}
L000189	\end{gather}
L000190	Making use of Eq. \eqref{psit} it is possible to construct two orthogonal solutions of Eq. \eqref{shrod} describing the whole phase interval  including the anticrossing region $\chi \approx \pi$. These solutions read
L000191	\begin{gather}
L000192	\tilde\Psi_{\mu} (t) = \sum_{\nu=\pm}\mathcal{S}_{\nu,\mu}(t) \psi_{\nu}(t),
L000193	\quad
L000194	\tilde \Psi_{\mu}^+(t) \tilde \Psi_{\tilde \mu}(t) = \delta_{\mu, \tilde \mu}.
L000195	\label{rddef}
L000196	\end{gather}
L000197	Here $\mathcal{S}_{\nu,\mu}(t)$ are elements of the unitary scattering matrix
L000198	\begin{equation}
L000199	\mathcal{S}(t)=
L000200	\begin{pmatrix}
L000201	r(t) & d(t) \\
L000202	-d^*(t) & r^*(t)
L000203	\end{pmatrix}, \quad  |r(t)|^2 + |d(t)|^2 = 1
L000204	\label{S}
L000205	\end{equation}
L000206	with the functions $r(t)$ and $d(t)$ to be determined below. For later convenience, we also define the phase
L000207	\begin{equation}
L000208	\delta(t)=\pi-\arg d(t)-\arg r(t).
L000209	\label{deltat}
L000210	\end{equation}
L000211	
L000212	Employing two linearly independent solutions $\tilde\Psi_{\mu} (t)$ of Eq. \eqref{shrod}, one readily finds the kernel of the operator
L000213	\begin{equation}
L000214	(\varepsilon - \mathcal{H})^{-1}
L000215	=
L000216	-i \theta(t - t')
L000217	\sum_{\mu =\pm}
L000218	\tilde \Psi_{\mu}(t)
L000219	\sqrt{\dfrac{\sin [\chi(t)/2]}{\sin [\chi(t')/2]}}
L000220	\tilde \Psi^+_{\mu}(t').
L000221	\label{invH}
L000222	\end{equation}
L000223	The integral kernel of $(\tilde W +  a)^{-1}$ then reads
L000224	\begin{multline}
L000225	(\tilde W +  a)^{-1}
L000226	=
L000227	i\dfrac{\sqrt{D}|\Delta|}{2}
L000228	\theta(t - t')
L000229	\sqrt{\sin [\chi(t)/2]\sin [\chi(t')/2]}
L000230	\\\times
L000231	\sum_{\mu, \nu, \tilde \nu}
L000232	\mathcal{S}_{\nu,\mu}(t) \psi_{\nu}(t)
L000233	\begin{pmatrix}
L000234	i  & -1 \\
L000235	1 & i
L000236	\end{pmatrix}
L000237	\dfrac{\mathcal{S}^*_{\tilde \nu,\mu}(t') \psi^+_{\tilde \nu}(t')}{a(\tilde \nu \varepsilon_A[\chi(t')])},
L000238	\label{inv11}
L000239	\end{multline}
L000240	where $\psi_{\nu}(t)  M  \psi^+_{\tilde \nu}(t')$ denotes a block matrix with entries $m_{ij} \psi_{\nu}(t)\psi^+_{\tilde \nu}(t')$.
L000241	
L000242	Substituting Eq. \eqref{inv11} into Eq. \eqref{T3}, making use of the exact relation
L000243	$\Psi^*_{\mu}(t) = \mu\hat  p_2 \Psi_{-\mu}(t) $ (enabling one to perform a summation over $\mu$), fixing $d(t) \to 0$ for $\chi \ll \pi$ by an appropriate choice of $\tilde\Psi_{\pm}(t)$ and restricting ourselves to the low temperature limit $T\ll |\Delta |$ we arrive at the general expression for CPR out of equilibrium
L000244	\begin{equation}
L000245	I(t)=
L000246	\tilde \Psi^+_+ (t)\hat J[\chi (t)] \tilde \Psi_+ (t),
L000247	\label{T7}
L000248	\end{equation}
L000249	where we defined the matrix current operator
L000250	\begin{equation}
L000251	\hat J (\chi)=
L000252	e D |\Delta|
L000253	\sin (\chi/2)
L000254	\begin{pmatrix}
L000255	\sqrt{D} & \sqrt{R} e^{i\chi/2 }  \\
L000256	\sqrt{R}e^{-i\chi/2 } & -\sqrt{D}
L000257	\end{pmatrix}.
L000258	\label{A}
L000259	\end{equation}
L000260	
L000261	{\it Andreev states evolution and Landau-Zener tunneling.} In order to evaluate the current $I(t)$ \eqref{T7} as a function of the phase $\chi (t)$ we now need to solve the Schr\"odinger-like equation \eqref{shrod} and to construct the ``wave function'' $\tilde\Psi_+ (t)$.  At low voltages $eV \ll |\Delta |$ and for the phase values $\chi (t)$ smaller than and not very close to $\pi$ one can set $d(t)=0$, which yields $\tilde\Psi_+(t) = \psi_+(t)$ in this region.
L000262	
L000263	Provided the phase values $\chi (t)$ become sufficiently close to $\pi$ the adiabatic approximation breaks down due to strong coupling between Andreev levels. At low voltages, the corresponding nonadiabatic dynamics of the Andreev levels is realized for weakly reflecting junctions $R\ll1$. Introducing dimensionless time variable $\tau = \sqrt{|\Delta|\dot\chi(t_{\pi})} (t-t_{\pi}) + (1/2)\sqrt{\dot\chi(t_{\pi})/|\Delta|}$
L000264	and the adiabadicity parameter $s = R|\Delta|/\dot\chi(t_{\pi})$, close to the point $\chi =\pi$
L000265	we can reduce Eq. \eqref{shrod} to the form
L000266	\begin{equation}
L000267	i \dfrac{\partial \tilde \Psi}{\partial \tau}
L000268	=
L000269	\begin{pmatrix}
L000270	-\tau/2  & \sqrt{s} \\
L000271	\sqrt{s} & \tau/2
L000272	\end{pmatrix}
L000273	\tilde \Psi ,
L000274	\label{shrod3}
L000275	\end{equation}
L000276	which is standard for the problem of Landau-Zener tunneling. The solution of this problem is well known \cite{Z32} and allows one to immediately determine the scattering matrix $\mathcal{S}$ \eqref{S}. In particular, for $\chi > \pi$ and sufficiently far from the anticrossing point $\chi=\pi$ one finds \cite{Z32,Vit96,Iv23}
L000277	\begin{gather}
L000278	\label{d}
L000279	d = - e^{-\pi s},
L000280	\\
L000281	\delta =
L000282	 -\frac{\pi}{4} + s\ln \frac{s}{e}  -  \arg \Gamma(is),
L000283	\label{delta}
L000284	\end{gather}
L000285	where $\Gamma (x)$ is the Euler gamma-function.
L000286	
L000287	{\it CPR and interference of Andreev states.} Now we are ready to evaluate the supercurrent $I$ across the junction. Combining Eqs.
L000288	\eqref{psit}-\eqref{deltat} with Eqs. \eqref{T7}-\eqref{A}, we get
L000289	\begin{multline}
L000290	I(t)=-2e
L000291	\dfrac{\partial \varepsilon_A(\chi)}{\partial \chi}\Biggl[
L000292	1 - 2|d(t)|^2
L000293	+
L000294	2  \sqrt{R} |r(t)| |d(t)|
L000295	\\\times
L000296	\tan [\chi(t)/2]
L000297	\cos\left( \delta(t)  + 2 \int_{t_{\pi}}^t\varepsilon_A[\chi(t')] d t' \right)
L000298	\Biggr].
L000299	\label{T+}
L000300	\end{multline}
L000301	Equation \eqref{T+} together with Eqs. \eqref{d}, \eqref{delta} represents the central result of our present work. It establishes a general relation between the current $I(t)$ and the time dependent Josephson phase $\chi (t)$ in superconducting junctions at sufficiently low bias voltages $eV \ll |\Delta |$ and low temperatures.
L000302	
L000303	Let us for a moment restrict ourselves to a simple step-like approximation for the probability of Landau-Zener tunneling
L000304	\begin{equation}
L000305	|d|^2\equiv 1-|r|^2=
L000306	\begin{cases}
L000307	0, & 0<\chi <\pi,
L000308	\\
L000309	e^{-\frac{\pi R|\Delta |}{eV}}, & \pi <\chi <2\pi
L000310	\end{cases}
L000311	\label{d2}
L000312	\end{equation}
L000313	and compare our result \eqref{T+} with that in Eq. \eqref{Av} \cite{AB1}. The key difference between these results lies in the presence of the oscillating contribution to the supercurrent $\propto |r||d|$ (second and third lines in Eq. \eqref{T+}) at  $\pi <\chi <2\pi$ which is totally absent in Eq. \eqref{Av}. The appearance of this contribution is easy to understand: After Landau-Zener tunneling event both Andreev states get partially occupied and exhibit quantum interference which manifests itself in the above oscillations. As follows from Eq. \eqref{T+}, the amplitude of these oscillations reaches its maximum at $|r|\approx |d| \approx 1/\sqrt{2}$ and vanishes in both limits $|r| \to 0$ and $|d| \to 0$. Thus, we predict a novel effect of coherent oscillations of the supercurrent due to quantum interference of Andreev states in superconducting junctions driven by a
L000314	(small) external voltage bias $V$.
L000315	
L000316	Note that the step-like approximation \eqref{d2} becomes insufficient in the vicinity of the anticrossing point $\chi =\pi$. In order to quantitatively describe the behavior $I[\chi (t)]$ close to this point it is necessary to perform a detailed calculation of the scattering matrix \eqref{S} which we relegate to End Matter. For the sake of comparison we also calculated CPR $I[\chi (t)]$ numerically employing exact recurrence relations \cite{KZ} equivalent to the expression \eqref{T3} in the limit of a constant in time bias voltage.
L000317	The numerical code used for these calculations is publicly available at \cite{Code}.
L000318	The corresponding numerically exact results are displayed in Figs. \ref{Ichi-R001-V003-fig}-\ref{Ichi-R001-V030-fig} together with the dependencies $I[\chi (t)]$ \eqref{T+} evaluated at $R=0.01$ and different values of $V$.
L000319	\begin{figure}
L000320	\begin{center}
L000321	\includegraphics[width=8cm]{Ichi-R001-V003-paper-fig}
L000322	\end{center}
L000323	\caption{The current $I$ in a single channel junction as a function of $\chi(t)$ for  $R=0.01$ and $eV=0.03 |\Delta|$. Black solid line represents a numerically exact result, while dashed blue line corresponds to Eq. \eqref{T+} with $r(t)$ and $d(t)$ obtained from the solution of the Zener problem (see End Matter). Inset: exact dependencies $d(t)$ and $\delta(t)$.}
L000324	\label{Ichi-R001-V003-fig}
L000325	\end{figure}
L000326	
L000327	Figure \ref{Ichi-R001-V003-fig} demonstrates CPR at a small constant voltage value $V=0.03|\Delta |/e$ in which case the amplitude of coherent oscillations is close to its maximum at a given value of $R$ being only few times smaller than the critical current value $I_c=e|\Delta |$ for our junction. We indeed clearly observe these oscillations within the phase interval $\pi  < \chi (t) <2\pi$ with the amplitude
L000328	decreasing monotonously as one goes away from the anticrossing point $\chi =\pi$. The oscillation frequency $\omega$ follows adiabatically the instantaneous level splitting as $\omega[\chi(t)]=2\varepsilon_A[\chi(t)]$. We also observe practically ideal agreement between analytic and numerical results for CPR except for small deviations between the two curves close to the points $\chi =0$, $2\pi$.
L000329	CPR of a similar form is also recovered at even smaller values of $V$ (not shown), with oscillations of a relatively smaller amplitude and practically invisible difference between the curves in the vicinity of the phase values $\chi =0$, $2\pi$.
L000330	\begin{figure}
L000331	\begin{center}
L000332	\includegraphics[width=8cm]{Ichi-R001-V010-paper-fig}
L000333	\end{center}
L000334	\caption{The same as in the main panel of Fig.~\ref{Ichi-R001-V003-fig} for $eV = 0.1 |\Delta|$.}
L000335	\label{Ichi-R001-V010-fig}
L000336	\end{figure}
L000337	
L000338	The same CPR dependencies evaluated both numerically and analytically at $V=0.1|\Delta |/e$ are displayed in Fig. \ref{Ichi-R001-V010-fig}. Coherent oscillations remain clearly visible at $\pi  < \chi (t) <2\pi$, although in a somewhat less pronounced form. A very good agreement between the two curves is again observed in the central part of the plot, though deviations between them towards the points $\chi =0,2\pi$  become somewhat bigger than in Fig. \ref{Ichi-R001-V003-fig}. This discrepancy increases further with increasing $V$, as it is demonstrated, e.g., in Fig. \ref{Ichi-R001-V030-fig}.
L000339	
L000340	The reason for this discrepancy is as follows. So far we have completely neglected the sub-Ohmic dissipative contribution to the current $\delta I$ which at low voltages $eV \ll |\Delta |$ yields the terms $\propto V^{2/3}$ in the $I-V$ curve \cite{GZ2} and $\propto V^{1/3}$ in CPR \cite{KZ,GZ3}. Furthermore, this dissipative contribution is mostly concentrated in the vicinity of the phase values $\chi = 2\pi k$ \cite{FN}, just where the deviations between Eq. \eqref{T+} and the numerical curve are observed.
L000341	
L000342	\begin{figure}
L000343	\begin{center}
L000344	\includegraphics[width=8cm]{Ichi-R001-V030-paper-fig}
L000345	\end{center}
L000346	\caption{The same as in the main panel of Fig.~\ref{Ichi-R001-V003-fig} for $eV = 0.3 |\Delta|$. The red dotted line represents the sum $I+\delta I$ defined by Eqs. \eqref{T+} and \eqref{diss}.}
L000347	\label{Ichi-R001-V030-fig}
L000348	\end{figure}
L000349	
L000350	In order to account for this extra contribution to the current, at sufficiently small values of both $R$ and $|r|$ we may use the result \cite{GZ3} obtained for fully transmitting junctions. In our notations this result takes the form
L000351	\begin{equation}
L000352	\delta I =\dfrac{be|\Delta|}{\pi}
L000353	\left(\dfrac{eV}{|\Delta|}\right)^{1/3}
L000354	\exp\left[
L000355	-0.27 b \left(\dfrac{|\Delta|}{eV}\right)^{1/3} |\delta\chi|
L000356	\right],
L000357	\label{diss}
L000358	\end{equation}
L000359	where $\delta\chi$ is the distance to the nearest point $2\pi n$, the parameter $b \simeq 2.5$  for $eV/|\Delta| \lesssim 0.01$ and $b \simeq 2.7 $  for $eV/|\Delta| \gtrsim 0.1$. In Fig. \ref{Ichi-R001-V030-fig} we also plotted the dependence $I(\chi)+\delta I(\chi)$ defined by Eqs. \eqref{T+} and \eqref{diss} which demonstrates a remarkable agreement with the numerical result at all values of $\chi$.
L000360	
L000361	We also note that the parameter values in Fig. \ref{Ichi-R001-V030-fig} match with the applicability range of the perturbative calculation \cite{GZ1} $R|\Delta | \ll eV \ll 2|\Delta |$. The results derived here are fully consistent with those of Ref. \onlinecite{GZ1} within their applicability range. In particular, we point out that no coherent oscillations in CPR were recovered within the perturbative approach \cite{GZ1}. The reason for that becomes clear from our Eq. \eqref{T+}: Although the current oscillations are still present in the perturbative regime, their magnitude is controlled by the terms $\sim R\sqrt{|\Delta /(eV)}$. These terms are parametrically smaller than the leading correction to the current  $\sim R|\Delta |/(eV)$ and, hence, the current oscillations were essentially ignored in \cite{GZ1}. Accordingly, these oscillations are hardly visible also in our Fig. \ref{Ichi-R001-V030-fig}.
L000362	
L000363	
L000364	{\it CPR in diffusive junctions.} Our results can be directly extended to junctions with many conducting channels. Of particular interest are superconducting junctions with sufficiently short diffusive barriers in which case the reflection coefficients $R$ of conducting channels
L000365	are controlled by the Dorokhov distribution \cite{Dor}
L000366	\begin{equation}
L000367	\rho(R) = \dfrac{\pi}{2 e^2 R_N} \dfrac{1}{(1-R)\sqrt{R}},
L000368	\label{Dor}
L000369	\end{equation}
L000370	where $R_N$ is the normal state resistance of a diffusive barrier. According to this bimodal distribution the barrier contains predominantly conducting channels with low and high transmission values. Averaging the expression for the current \eqref{T+} with the distribution \eqref{Dor} one should bear in mind that for the former group of channels it suffices to set $|d | \to 0$. Then one finds
L000371	\begin{multline}
L000372	I[\chi (t)]=\dfrac{\pi |\Delta |}{2eR_N}\Biggl\{\ln \left[\frac{1+\sin [\chi (t) /2]}{1-\sin [\chi (t)/2]}\right]\cos[\chi(t)/2]
L000373	\\+\sqrt{\dfrac{eV}{|\Delta|}}f[\chi (t)]+\dfrac{eV}{|\Delta|}g[\chi (t)]\Biggr\}.
L000374	\label{diff}
L000375	\end{multline}
L000376	The combination in the first line of Eq. \eqref{diff} represents the standard CPR for a diffusive superconducting junction in equilibrium at low temperatures $T \ll |\Delta |$ \cite{KO,ZP85} which -- as we demonstrated here -- also applies out of equilibrium at small bias voltages $eV \ll |\Delta |$.
L000377	
L000378	Additional contributions in the second line of Eq. \eqref{diff} originate from the terms in Eq. \eqref{T+} describing Landau-Zener tunneling
L000379	in which case only channels with $R \ll 1$ need to be taken into account. In particular, the contribution to the current $\propto \sqrt{V}$ in Eq. \eqref{diff} follows directly from the term $\propto |d|^2$ in Eq. \eqref{T+}. Accordingly, we have $f(\chi )=0$ for $0<\chi \lesssim \pi$ and $f(\chi )=2\sin (\chi /2)$ for $\pi\lesssim \chi <2\pi$ with a smooth crossover between these two regimes within a narrow region $|\chi-\pi|\sim \sqrt{|eV|/|\Delta|}$. The oscillating term in Eq. \eqref{T+} yields the last contribution $\propto V$ in Eq. \eqref{diff} and
L000380	$g(\chi )$ is an oscillating function containing terms proportional to $\cos \gamma (t)$ and $\sin\gamma (t)$ with $\gamma (t)=[2|\Delta|/(eV)]
L000381	[1 - \sin[(\chi(t)/2)]$.
L000382	
L000383	Averaging Eq. \eqref{diff} over time, we obtain
L000384	\begin{equation}
L000385	\overline{I} =\dfrac{|\Delta|}{eR_N}\sqrt{\dfrac{eV}{|\Delta |}},
L000386	\end {equation}
L000387	i.e. at small voltages and $T \to 0$ the differential conductance of short diffusive superconducting junctions behaves as $d\overline{I}/dV \propto 1/\sqrt{V}$.
L000388	
L000389	
L000390	In summary, we microscopically derived an effective Schr\"odinger-like equation which describes quantum dynamics of Andreev states in superconducting nanojunctions out of equilibrium. By solving this equation one can find the "wave functions" corresponding to these states and evaluate electric current flowing across the junction. In the presence of an external voltage bias Landau-Zener tunneling occurs between Andreev levels. As a result, both Andreev states become occupied and start interfere with each other. Such quantum interference, in turn, may result in pronounced coherent oscillations of the current as a function of the time dependent Josephson phase $\chi (t)$. This effect has further implications for junctions with many conducting channels and can be directly tested in future experiments.
